<section class="chapter" aria-label="Governance and system definitions">
  <div class="chapter__inner">

    <h2 class="section-title">Governance & System Definitions</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      Autonomy is not assumed. Safe automation depends on clear decision rights,
      stable definitions, and explicit thresholds so systems remain accountable
      and consistent across tools.
    </p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 0 — Monitor</b>
        <span>Measure, detect drift, and surface risks. No action.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 1 — Auto-execute</b>
        <span>Low-risk reminders, routing, hygiene, and updates. Logged and visible.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 2 — Recommend</b>
        <span>Actions that affect outcomes require explicit human approval.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 3 — Escalate</b>
        <span>High-stakes decisions always route to humans. Governance applies.</span>
      </div>

    </div>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">

      <a class="mini" href="/system/metrics/dictionary/">
        <b>Definitions</b>
        <span>Shared meanings for signals and metrics so comparisons stay valid.</span>
      </a>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Logic</b>
        <span>Interpretation rules ensure two people don't reach two different conclusions.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Thresholds</b>
        <span>Explicit triggers for alerts, automation, escalation, or human review.</span>
      </div>

